<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 900px; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 20px;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; margin-top: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Book Is on the Table. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; color: #555;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Book Is on the Table.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Respond to written questions from peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts about classroom items using simple phrases and sentences (e.g., "This is a pencil."). Can respond to written questions from peers about classroom topics (e.g., "Where is the book?" "It is near the teacher."). Can write a short note to a peer using familiar language (e.g., "Hello, Elena! Your bag is on the floor.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards or realia of classroom objects (e.g., a book, a pencil, a desk, a chair). Students are asked to point to the object and say its name. Then, the teacher says, "Look around the classroom! What do you see?" and encourages students to shout out names of objects. The teacher then asks students to write down three classroom objects they see on small pieces of paper or mini-whiteboards (e.g., "desk, chair, book").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a book and places it on a table, saying clearly, "The book is on the table." The teacher writes the sentence on the board. Then, the teacher places a pencil in a pencil case, saying, "The pencil is in the pencil case." and writes it. The teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table", exaggerating the sounds. The teacher also explicitly blends the sounds for prepositions, e.g., /ɔ/ /-n/ for "on", showing how they combine.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks, "Is the book under the table?" (Expected answer: "No, it's on.") "Is the pencil on the pencil case?" (Expected answer: "No, it's in.") The teacher provides an example: "This is a book. The book is on the desk." and asks students to repeat the sentence chorally and individually, focusing on pronunciation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short note or a simple description of a classroom, presented on a large chart paper or projected, including pictures of the described items. The genre is a short descriptive text, and the register is informal and simple, appropriate for describing everyday objects. The format is a brief paragraph or a list of sentences describing locations.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and interpret simple written descriptions of object locations within a familiar context (the classroom).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a classroom with items placed in specific positions. Below the picture, there is a short text: <br> "Hello! This is our classroom. <br> The board is on the wall. <br> The teacher's desk is next to the board. <br> My chair is behind my desk. <br> The book is on my desk. <br> My pencil is in my pencil case." <br> The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to the words, then asks students to read along silently. Students are given a printed copy or look at the projected text.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected: "Our classroom.") "What words tell us where things are?" (Expected: "on, next to, behind, in").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students circle 'T' or 'F' for sentences like: "The book is on the shelf." (F – based on the text). "The board is on the wall." (T).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students draw a line from an object (e.g., "pencil") to its location (e.g., "pencil case") based on the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple declarative sentences about object locations using prepositions of place (in, on, next to, between, behind) and WH-questions ("Where is...?"). Using vocabulary: desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map; and possessive adjectives (my, your).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-blanks:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with sentences about classroom items, each with a blank for a preposition. Students look at pictures of items in various locations (e.g., a crayon in a pencil case, a ruler on a notebook) and complete the sentences. <br> Examples: "The crayon is _____ the pencil case." "The ruler is _____ the notebook." "The board is _____ the shelf and the door."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Builders:</b> Students receive another worksheet with sentence frames and image prompts. For each image (e.g., a map on the wall, an eraser next to a pencil), students choose words from a word bank to complete sentences. <br> Example: Image of a map on the wall. Word bank: [map, wall, on, is, the]. Student writes: "The map is on the wall." <br> Image of an eraser next to a pencil. Word bank: [eraser, pencil, next to, is, the]. Student writes: "The eraser is next to the pencil."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check - Written Questions and Answers:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student draws two classroom items in specific locations on a piece of paper (e.g., "my book on my chair," "your pencil in your pencil case"). They then write two "Where is...?" questions about their drawings. Students exchange papers with their partner and write the answers to their partner's questions using full sentences (e.g., Partner A writes: "Where is the book?" Partner B writes: "It is on your chair."). The teacher monitors, providing support and correcting written errors, especially related to prepositions and simple sentence structure.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product - Students will write a short note to a classmate about a misplaced item, or a short description of their desk area.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b> Students imagine they found a classmate's item (e.g., "pencil," "notebook," "eraser") in a specific place in the classroom. They will write a short note to that peer, telling them about the item and its location. <br> The teacher provides a template or a model on the board: <br> "Hello, [Peer's Name]! <br> Your [item name] is [preposition] the [place]. <br> It is [adjective, e.g., small, blue]. <br> See you! <br> [Your Name]" <br> For example: "Hello, Elena! Your pencil is under my desk. It is yellow. See you! Juan." <br> Alternatively, students can write 3-4 sentences describing the items on and around their own desk, focusing on accurate use of prepositions and vocabulary (e.g., "This is my desk. My book is on my desk. My pencil case is next to the book. My chair is behind my desk."). Students then exchange their notes/descriptions with another peer who reads and confirms understanding. The teacher circulates, offering support and ensuring the writing aligns with the prompt and incorporates the target language.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' writing during Stage 4.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written notes or desk descriptions from Stage 4. Additionally, students perform a quick peer check by exchanging their written notes/descriptions and giving one positive comment and one suggestion for improvement (e.g., "Good sentences!" "Maybe add another object.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: square; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (classroom items) (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of prepositions of place (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Formation of simple sentences (Subject-Verb-Object/Complement) (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the message in the note/description (Yes/No)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small piece of paper before leaving: <br> 1. "Today I can write about..." <br> 2. "One new word I used is..."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and Teacher reflection)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about the classroom was easy for me?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was a little difficult?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked well to encourage students to compose short texts? Was the peer interaction for writing effective? Did students successfully use prepositions of place in their written output? What adjustments are needed for sentence complexity or vocabulary review in the next lesson to support students who struggled with forming simple sentences or using specific prepositions like "between" or "behind"? Perhaps more visual aids or sentence frames for weaker writers are needed.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing practice will prepare students for the final project in Lesson 5, where they will integrate all skills (listening, reading, speaking, writing) to complete a collaborative task, likely involving creating a 'Classroom Treasure Map' or a 'Classroom Rules' poster which requires describing locations and objects.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>